The Carlsson–Stein dynamic chromospheric model predicts that acoustic waves steepen into shocks, producing strong, quasi-periodic temperature fluctuations. Synthetic continuum emission at 0.8–5.0 mm reproduces these fluctuations as brightness-temperature oscillations, with longer wavelengths generally sampling higher layers and showing larger oscillation amplitudes. Because millimeter free–free emission is close to LTE and the Rayleigh–Jeans limit applies, brightness temperature is a comparatively direct measure of the local electron temperature.

BIMA quiet-Sun observations at about 3.5 mm detected time-dependent brightness variations and oscillatory power on chromospheric timescales, broadly consistent with the model’s shock-driven behavior, although spatial averaging reduced the observed amplitudes.

Together, the modeling and observations show that 0.8–5.0 mm measurements provide:

- direct thermal diagnostics of chromospheric oscillations;
- sensitivity to shocks and acoustic-wave propagation;
- height discrimination through multiwavelength sampling;
- a way to measure changes in oscillation amplitude and phase with altitude.

Thus, high-cadence, high-resolution millimeter observations can provide a tomographic view of solar atmospheric dynamics, especially with modern interferometers such as ALMA.
